# ABOUTME: Declares what `use IO::Socket::IP` installs: Socket's 170 default names and its own :export_ok names, re-exported
# ABOUTME: through IO::Socket's own `use Socket;`, plus its own OO methods. Measured on perl 5.42.0 with IO::Socket::IP 0.43.

package IO::Socket::IP;

# IO::Socket::IP defines its own import (IP.pm:122-139), which reads
# only its leading "-register" option -- registering
# IO::Socket::IP::_ForINET as the AF_INET/AF_INET6 domain handler --
# and forwards everything else, with "-register" filtered out, to
# IO::Socket's own import, which in turn forwards to Socket's own
# Exporter: measured on 5.42.0, a bare `use IO::Socket::IP;` and a
# `use IO::Socket::IP -register;` both install exactly the 170 names
# Socket's own bare `use Socket;` does (Socket.tpm's :export set), with
# Socket's own prototypes, types and :listop parses, copied here
# unchanged. Declaring import here, with no scalar key after the
# invocant (same shape as Moose.tpm's own, perigrin's ruling,
# 2026-10-20: "the parameters are the signature to import()"), states
# that any list, "-register" included, still installs the same 170
# names a bare `use IO::Socket::IP;` does.
sub import (Str $class: List @args) Void;

# 161 of the 170 live only in IO::Socket's own package, not in
# IO::Socket::IP's (measured: IO::Socket.pm's file-scope `use Socket;`
# installs them there; IO::Socket::IP->can(name) finds them by
# inheritance, but IO::Socket::IP::name itself does not exist). The
# return-type oracle's sub-form call looks up Module::name directly, so
# it cannot reach any of these 161 under this module -- internal/infer's
# TestOOModulesReturnTypes exempts them by name, and Socket.tpm's own
# oracle (TestXSLibrariesReturnTypes) already holds their return types.
#
# The other nine -- AF_INET, sockaddr_family, SO_REUSEADDR,
# SO_REUSEPORT, SO_BROADCAST, SO_ERROR, SOCK_DGRAM, SOCK_STREAM and
# SOL_SOCKET -- are also imported directly into IO::Socket::IP's own
# package, by its own file-scope `use Socket ... qw(...)` (measured:
# IO::Socket::IP::AF_INET is defined after a bare `require
# IO::Socket::IP`), so the oracle's sub-form call reaches them here
# directly; TestOOModulesReturnTypes holds them with oracle/IO-Socket-IP.pl's
# own call cases, not exempt.
